<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shinkolobwe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shinkolobwe is a uranium and radium mine in the Haut-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, discovered in 1915 and worked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-miniere-du-haut-katanga">Union Minière du Haut Katanga</a>. Its exceptionally rich ore supplied most of the uranium used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a>. The mine was officially closed after independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shinkolobwe.jpg" alt="Shinkolobwe" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chalux · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shinkolobwe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The mine, also called Kasolo, Chinkolobew or Shainkolobwe, lies 20 km west of Likasi (formerly Jadotville), 20 km south of Kambove and about 145 km north-west of Lubumbashi, and takes its name from the long-gone nearby village of Shinkolobwe. The English geologist Robert Rich Sharp (1881-1960) found the deposits. Uranium minerals, with cobalt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, nickel, bismuth and arsenic, occur in veinlets along fractures in the dolomitic shales of the Precambrian Mine Series, where uraninite mineralisation occurred 630 million years ago. Many new minerals were first identified there, among them becquerelite, curite, soddyite and sharpite.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first mine opened in the Belgian Congo in 1921, and only the richest ore was sent to Olen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> for the extraction of radium and uranium, the rest being held in reserve. Open-cut mining was suspended in 1936. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> showed interest in the Belgian stock of ore in 1939, but after the Nazis occupied Belgium in 1940 they took 1,200 tons of it, most of which the Alsos Mission recovered in 1945.</p><p>Edgar Sengier, director of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-miniere-du-haut-katanga">Union Minière du Haut Katanga</a>, had stockpiled 1,200 tonnes of ore in a warehouse on Staten Island, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Colonel Ken Nichols bought it, with another 3,000 tonnes stored above ground at the mine, for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a>, and after the agreement of September 1942 an average of 400 tons of uranium oxide a month was shipped to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The mine was reopened, its location removed from maps and journalists kept away.</p><p>After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> received 1,440 tons of uranium concentrates from the Belgian Congo in 1947, 2,792 in 1951 and 1,600 in 1953, and a garrison guarded the site, supported by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> base at Kamina. With new uranium mines opening in the United States and the Congo becoming independent, the Americans left in 1960 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-miniere-du-haut-katanga">Union Minière</a> sealed the mine with concrete.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The mine was officially closed by presidential decree on 28 January 2004. In July 2004 part of the old mine collapsed, killing eight people and injuring thirteen. Industrial production has ceased and cement lids seal the shafts, but a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> report of June 2006 found that artisanal mining was still going on there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shinkolobwe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Union Minière du Haut Katanga mined copper and cobalt, and uranium from <b>Shinkolobwe</b> supplied the Manhattan Project.</blockquote><a href="/state/belgian-congo">Belgian Congo</a></li></ul></section>
